WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:06.480
Grisly gore-fests, dance routines… and Iron&nbsp;
Man doing laundry? Even the best movies shed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:11.240
a scene or two in the cutting room — and&nbsp;
these sci-fi classics are no exception.

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:15.160
One of the most celebrated science fiction&nbsp;
movies of the early '80s, Star Trek II:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:19.160
The Wrath of Khan may have saved the franchise&nbsp;
after the disappointment of the first movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:24.634
a few years earlier. But it almost included a&nbsp;
subplot that could have hurt its critical success.

00:00:24.634 --> 00:00:26.887
"Khaaaan!"

00:00:27.026 --> 00:00:30.400
In addition to a well-known deleted&nbsp;
scene involving Scotty's nephew,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:34.720
who is killed after Khan's initial attack&nbsp;
on the Enterprise, the movie also cut out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:39.440
an entire character: Khan's infant&nbsp;
son. In early versions of the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:45.160
the baby is introduced when Chekov and Captain&nbsp;
Terrell are searching Khan's ship, the Botany Bay.

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:50.040
The child would later appear with Khan aboard&nbsp;
the USS Reliant; in another deleted scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:55.200
he dies when Khan accidentally activates the&nbsp;
Genesis device. Considering that the studio&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:59.320
decided to add a more positive ending late&nbsp;
in production, it could be that they felt the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:04.520
death of Scotty's nephew and Khan's infant son&nbsp;
seemed a bit grim for a PG-rated sci-fi family&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.520 --> 00:01:10.840
film. Images of Khan's son were revealed for the&nbsp;
first time in a 1982 issue of Starblazer Magazine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:14.920
but full footage of the scenes in&nbsp;
question have never been released.

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:20.120
Basically all movies end up having a few scenes&nbsp;
cut here and there — in the case of Event Horizon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:24.920
however, there's actually an entirely different&nbsp;
cut that has been lost to time. This version of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:30.200
the film reportedly runs over two solid hours,&nbsp;
as opposed to the 95-minute theatrical release,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:34.600
and includes far more gore. Tragically,&nbsp;
the studio didn't feel that the original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:38.560
footage was worth keeping and much of&nbsp;
it has never seen the light of day.

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:42.080
Some excised moments include Captain&nbsp;
Miller discovering a loose tooth in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:47.240
corridor — apparently the remains of a former&nbsp;
crewman. Most of the deleted moments, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:51.760
were extended versions of existing scenes that&nbsp;
were far more grisly than the studio or test&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:56.720
audiences could handle. A few of these managed to&nbsp;
survive, and a canister containing the original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:01.680
film was discovered in an old Transylvania salt&nbsp;
mine. They eventually made their way onto a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:07.280
later DVD release in a raw, unfinished form. Not&nbsp;
all of the deleted sequences could be restored,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:11.920
though, as the newly-discovered film&nbsp;
was in poor condition when it was found.

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:15.640
Director David Cronenberg made a name&nbsp;
for himself as the go-to filmmaker for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:21.080
body horror thanks to early cult classics&nbsp;
such as Videodrome and Scanners. In 1985,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:26.770
he was given the task of remaking the '50s B-movie&nbsp;
The Fly. With Jeff Goldblum in the title role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.770 --> 00:02:31.600
Cronenberg crafted one of the most gruesome&nbsp;
mainstream science fiction movies ever made.

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:36.000
"You look bad. You smell bad."

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:37.960
"Never been much of a bather."

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:39.680
But, if it hadn't been cut,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:43.680
one scene in particular would have made&nbsp;
the whole film even harder to stomach.

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:48.320
The scene in question takes place after Seth&nbsp;
Brundle has begun his transformation. Looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:52.400
for a way to reverse the effects, Brundle thinks&nbsp;
he's found the answer, and proceeds to test out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:57.920
an experimental procedure on a baboon and a cat.&nbsp;
However, the process inadvertently amalgamates the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:03.240
two animals, and Brundle is forced to beat it to&nbsp;
death to spare it the agony of its own existence.

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:06.760
This moment was actually included&nbsp;
in early UK screenings of the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:12.040
but movie-goers were so horrified that the studio&nbsp;
chose to cut it for wide release. It wasn't until&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:17.920
the film's 20th anniversary that it turned up&nbsp;
again, this time on a deluxe Blu-ray release.

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:22.320
There's no end to the number of great deleted&nbsp;
scenes in the Marvel Cinematic Universe. Most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:26.360
of them have found their way out of the vault in&nbsp;
one form or another, and Marvel loves including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.360 --> 00:03:32.160
as many as possible on their home video releases.&nbsp;
Nevertheless, for 2008's Iron Man, some deleted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:37.040
scenes will never be released — not if Kevin&nbsp;
Feige has anything to say about it, that is.

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:41.080
In an interview with Screen Rant, Marvel&nbsp;
executive producer Jeremy Latcham revealed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.080 --> 00:03:45.720
some deleted scenes were deemed so atrocious&nbsp;
that they'll never be made public. One of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:50.200
sees Tony Stark helping prison guard&nbsp;
Abu do some chores. Latcham revealed:

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:53.880
"Tony is doing laundry for the captors, but&nbsp;
he's really breaking the washing machine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:58.720
and stealing parts from it to build the&nbsp;
Mark 1 suit. It's so absurd. It's wild."

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:00.720
The scene was apparently so poorly made that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:04.520
studio boss Feige never wants&nbsp;
it shown. Latcham continued:

00:04:04.520 --> 00:04:09.560
"I remember Kevin saying to me in 2012, [...] 'We&nbsp;
can never put out Abu doing laundry. People will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:13.960
know we don't know what we're doing. It'll&nbsp;
be embarrassing if they see these scenes.'"

00:04:13.960 --> 00:04:20.400
Starship Troopers is a sci-fi action classic from&nbsp;
1997 — and, in some ways, it's also a romance. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:25.160
love quadrangle between Rico, Carmen, Zander,&nbsp;
and Dizzy makes up a major part of the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.160 --> 00:04:29.400
especially in its early stages. Yet, if&nbsp;
director Paul Verhoeven had gotten his way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:34.160
the movie would have had an even greater emphasis&nbsp;
on the romantic angle of the story. In the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:39.280
much of it wound up deleted from the film after&nbsp;
test audiences didn't react well to the subplot.

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:43.360
Some of the scenes cut include a moment in&nbsp;
which Carmen deflects Rico's sexual advances,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:48.080
as well as a farewell scene where it becomes clear&nbsp;
that their relationship is somewhat forbidden.

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:52.880
In one later scene, after surviving a deadly&nbsp;
attack, Zander tells Carmen that he loves her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:57.480
helping to reinforce that new relationship&nbsp;
in a way the final film never does.

00:04:57.480 --> 00:05:00.680
The last deleted scene comes right&nbsp;
at the end of the film. We'd seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.680 --> 00:05:04.880
the death of Dizzy and Zander earlier on, and&nbsp;
audiences may have wondered what that meant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:08.880
for Johnny and Carmen's relationship.&nbsp;
Well, this scene answers that question,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:12.800
as the pair share a passionate&nbsp;
kiss just before the credits roll.

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:16.680
If you're looking for the best movie about&nbsp;
a robot cop, well, you're not gonna do much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:22.080
better than RoboCop. Die-hard fans consider it&nbsp;
the perfect '80s action movie, with not a moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:26.720
wasted in capturing the excitement of mortally&nbsp;
wounded cop Alex Murphy as he's transformed into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:32.280
the heroic cyborg known as RoboCop. As it turned&nbsp;
out, finding perfection involved trimming a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:37.640
moments from the original script, one of which&nbsp;
saw the hero cop returning to his own grave.

00:05:37.640 --> 00:05:41.240
Though it wasn't filmed, this scene was&nbsp;
included in both the novelization and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:45.360
the movie's Marvel Comics adaptation.&nbsp;
And when it came time for a sequel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:49.480
RoboCop 2, director Irvin Kershner&nbsp;
decided to make it happen. This time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.480 --> 00:05:54.000
the sequence was intended to be part of an early&nbsp;
moment carrying over from the first film's end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:58.800
when RoboCop embraces his identity as Alex&nbsp;
Murphy. Despite all the effort put into making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:03.760
the scene a reality, however, the graveyard&nbsp;
visit didn't make it into this movie either.

00:06:03.760 --> 00:06:08.760
Directed by the legendary Stanley Kubrick,&nbsp;
2001: A Space Odyssey is one of the seminal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:13.840
works in the sci-fi genre. It reshaped science&nbsp;
fiction with a mix of thought-provoking drama,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:17.320
nail-biting suspense, and&nbsp;
state-of-the-art special effects.

00:06:17.320 --> 00:06:20.680
"Open the pod bay doors, Hal."

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:24.960
"I'm sorry, Dave. I'm afraid I can't do that."

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:29.800
For years, rumors have claimed that Kubrick&nbsp;
trimmed some 20 minutes of footage from 2001,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.800 --> 00:06:34.400
and we finally got confirmation when reels from&nbsp;
the film were discovered in, of all places,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:39.440
another salt mine. These scenes aren't exactly the&nbsp;
most pivotal, but they do serve to flesh out the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:44.280
finer details of the mission to Jupiter. Some of&nbsp;
the footage includes extended sequences of Frank&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:49.360
Poole jogging, as well as an alternate version&nbsp;
of the iconic opening sequence. One deleted scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.360 --> 00:06:54.560
sees HAL 9000 ending the radio communication&nbsp;
between the Discovery and Poole's pod not long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.560 --> 00:06:59.400
before he's killed, which means a later exchange&nbsp;
between Dave and HAL makes a little more sense.

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:04.480
Set in the 23rd century, The Fifth Element&nbsp;
introduces Milla Jovovich as Leeloo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.480 --> 00:07:09.520
a woman believed by an order of ancient monks&nbsp;
to be the key to defeating a cosmic evil.

00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:14.200
One of The Fifth Element's most intriguing deleted&nbsp;
scenes takes place when Dallas is ferrying Leeloo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.200 --> 00:07:19.160
and the monk Cornelius to the airport. There, they&nbsp;
would have encountered a group of striking alien&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.160 --> 00:07:24.120
garbage collectors taking part in a fight with&nbsp;
local police. Remnants of this scene can be found&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.120 --> 00:07:28.800
in the final cut, as the airport itself is filled&nbsp;
with garbage piled meters high that is never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:34.880
adequately explained — the airport attendant only&nbsp;
briefly apologizes for "the mess." Years later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:40.920
a behind-the-scenes featurette on the movie's&nbsp;
4K UHD Blu-ray chronicled this lost moment.

00:07:40.920 --> 00:07:44.200
The last film in the franchise to&nbsp;
feature the cast of The Next Generation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.200 --> 00:07:49.880
the 2002 box office dud Star Trek: Nemesis was&nbsp;
met with a critical drubbing on its release.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.880 --> 00:07:53.680
Critics claimed the movie was dull,&nbsp;
lifeless, and lacking in the charm of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.680 --> 00:07:58.080
the TV series. But quite a few deleted scenes&nbsp;
would have gone a long way to keeping what was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.080 --> 00:08:02.520
lost — mostly smaller character moments between&nbsp;
the likes of Captain Picard, Commander Riker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:07.280
and Dr. Crusher. While fans have known about&nbsp;
some of these deleted scenes for decades,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:11.480
one recently surfaced that was never&nbsp;
included on any home video release.

00:08:11.480 --> 00:08:16.320
This scene comes after Data's death. Aboard&nbsp;
the Enterprise, which remains under repair,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:20.640
we find Worf and Geordi going through their&nbsp;
late comrade's quarters. At one point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:26.120
Data's cat, Spot, leaps into Worf's arms, who&nbsp;
reluctantly adopts the feline as his own. Cut&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.120 --> 00:08:30.080
from the film's initial DVD release despite&nbsp;
the inclusion of other deleted moments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.080 --> 00:08:33.680
this scene has since surfaced&nbsp;
online in low resolution.

00:08:33.680 --> 00:08:37.520
The biggest sci-fi movie of the '90s,&nbsp;
Independence Day was a watershed flick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.520 --> 00:08:41.880
that forever changed the summer blockbuster.&nbsp;
The movie isn't without its plot holes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.880 --> 00:08:45.800
however; many fans have raised questions&nbsp;
about the virus created by David Levinson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.800 --> 00:08:52.030
which he eventually uses to&nbsp;
take down the alien mothership.

00:08:52.030 --> 00:08:53.590
"Welcome to Earth."

00:08:53.840 --> 00:08:57.480
Some have wondered how his terrestrial&nbsp;
laptop could so easily interface with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.480 --> 00:09:00.680
technology from another world, while&nbsp;
others have scratched their heads at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.680 --> 00:09:04.200
how he even knew how to take down&nbsp;
their computer system at all.

00:09:04.200 --> 00:09:08.240
A deleted scene included on the film's 20th&nbsp;
anniversary home video release would have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.240 --> 00:09:12.600
answered one of those questions. In this&nbsp;
scene, Levinson lays out his plan to crash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.600 --> 00:09:16.400
the alien computer network in more detail&nbsp;
while he's given a tour of the captured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.400 --> 00:09:21.400
alien ship by Dr. Brackish Okun. While Okun&nbsp;
admits they have very little idea as to how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.400 --> 00:09:25.360
the alien technology works, Levinson&nbsp;
deduces that a sequential repeating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.360 --> 00:09:29.520
pattern they've discovered is actually a code&nbsp;
that they're using to coordinate their ships.

00:09:29.520 --> 00:09:32.840
It might not be the most air-tight&nbsp;
explanation, but it offers at least a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.840 --> 00:09:37.240
slightly better explanation as to how&nbsp;
Levinson brought down those aliens.

00:09:37.240 --> 00:09:41.560
Before the likes of Independence Day and Star&nbsp;
Wars turned the genre into blockbuster fare,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.560 --> 00:09:45.200
science fiction films were thoughtful,&nbsp;
talky dramas — and The Day the Earth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.200 --> 00:09:49.560
Stood Still was one of the very best. In&nbsp;
this eerie tale, an alien visitor named&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.560 --> 00:09:53.400
Klaatu arrives with a dire message for&nbsp;
Earth's leaders — one that he will not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.400 --> 00:09:57.800
reveal until they can all gather together&nbsp;
peacefully. Escaping government custody,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.800 --> 00:10:02.880
Klaatu tries to blend in with humanity in order to&nbsp;
learn why they are so distrustful of one another.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.880 --> 00:10:07.680
But there's one scene cut from the film that most&nbsp;
of the movie's fans have probably never seen.

00:10:07.680 --> 00:10:11.240
In the film, a detective arrives to&nbsp;
take Klaatu to see a local scientist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.240 --> 00:10:15.320
Professor Barnhardt. In the original script,&nbsp;
the detective first takes him to the police&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.320 --> 00:10:20.040
station for questioning. While there, a local&nbsp;
man is brought in seriously injured, and it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.040 --> 00:10:25.040
revealed that the man was attacked after he was&nbsp;
mistaken for Klaatu. Feeling a sense of guilt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.040 --> 00:10:29.880
but also confusion as to why people would attack&nbsp;
a stranger in such a manner, Klaatu begs for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.880 --> 00:10:34.320
police to take him straight to Barnhardt. Though&nbsp;
a brief scene, it's one that adds important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.320 --> 00:10:40.080
context to Klaatu's journey, demonstrating&nbsp;
both his empathy and humanity's folly.

00:10:40.080 --> 00:10:44.240
Blade Runner is pretty much a deleted scene&nbsp;
bonanza. Over the years, most of the scenes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.240 --> 00:10:47.960
dropped from the original release have found&nbsp;
their way into a multitude of different cuts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.960 --> 00:10:52.590
including the workprint cut, the director's&nbsp;
cut, and the so-called "Final Cut."

00:10:52.590 --> 00:11:00.400
"Like tears… in rain."

00:11:00.400 --> 00:11:04.360
Yet none of these versions include the&nbsp;
alternate, deleted opening scene. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.360 --> 00:11:09.040
because it was never actually filmed; in fact,&nbsp;
it was only revealed years after the fact in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.040 --> 00:11:13.480
series of storyboards, first published in&nbsp;
Future Noir: The Making of Blade Runner.

00:11:13.480 --> 00:11:17.040
In the alternate first scene, Detective&nbsp;
Rick Deckard is seen landing his spinner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.040 --> 00:11:21.760
on a desolate road. He exits the flying car&nbsp;
and enters an empty home, where he notices&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.760 --> 00:11:26.480
that there is still a pot of soup boiling on&nbsp;
the stove. As he listlessly stirs the soup,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.480 --> 00:11:31.520
a large, hulking man walks in — and Deckard&nbsp;
immediately shoots him dead. He then rips out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.520 --> 00:11:35.640
a piece of his jaw, revealing the man to be&nbsp;
a replicant, and takes off in his spinner.

00:11:35.640 --> 00:11:39.360
If all of this sounds familiar, it should.&nbsp;
That's because this original opening was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.360 --> 00:11:45.880
used as the intro to the sequel, Blade Runner&nbsp;
2049, which was released nearly 40 years later.

00:11:45.880 --> 00:11:50.360
Featuring some of the most jaw-dropping action&nbsp;
sequences in movie history, Jurassic Park is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.360 --> 00:11:56.000
true blockbuster adventure in every way. Steven&nbsp;
Spielberg's classic left indelible images etched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.000 --> 00:12:00.360
into the minds of countless movie-goers: whether&nbsp;
it's the first sight of the brontosauruses or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.360 --> 00:12:05.200
the clicking talons of the velociraptor on that&nbsp;
cold kitchen floor. No matter how many memorable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.200 --> 00:12:09.920
moments the movie boasts, however, there are&nbsp;
still more that didn't make it into the film.

00:12:09.920 --> 00:12:14.000
One deleted action sequence would have&nbsp;
seen Alan Grant, Tim, and Lex stumble upon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.000 --> 00:12:18.480
a sleeping Tyrannosaurus rex while roaming the&nbsp;
jungle. After sneaking past the beast without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.480 --> 00:12:22.560
drawing its attention, they make their way to a&nbsp;
nearby structure where they are able to grab an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.560 --> 00:12:27.800
inflatable raft from storage. They take off down&nbsp;
the river — but, just when the trio thinks they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.800 --> 00:12:32.880
in the clear, the T-Rex bursts out from behind&nbsp;
the trees and tears after them. It doesn't seem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.880 --> 00:12:37.901
as if this sequence was ever filmed, sadly,&nbsp;
and today it exists only in storyboard form.

00:12:38.923 --> 00:12:40.896
"We got one!"

00:12:42.920 --> 00:12:47.920
A supernatural comedy with a pair of former SNL&nbsp;
stars leading the way, Ghostbusters defied the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.920 --> 00:12:53.360
odds to become a pop culture icon. The movie&nbsp;
quickly inspired a diehard group of fans who,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.360 --> 00:12:58.360
for decades, only had a pair of films to pour&nbsp;
over. So you can imagine the excitement when,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.360 --> 00:13:03.440
in 2017, new footage from the original movie&nbsp;
was discovered — including some hilarious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.440 --> 00:13:07.760
deleted scenes. Found in the personal&nbsp;
collection of director Ivan Reitman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.760 --> 00:13:11.400
the reels' discovery caused quite&nbsp;
a stir among Ghostbusters fans.

00:13:11.400 --> 00:13:16.080
One of these scenes was "Fort Detmerring," a&nbsp;
long-known but never-seen moment in which Ray&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.080 --> 00:13:20.880
Stantz has a sexual encounter with a ghost. In&nbsp;
the final film, remnants of this scene are shown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.880 --> 00:13:26.240
in a montage, but the full sequence has never been&nbsp;
revealed. It opens with the Ecto-1 arriving at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.240 --> 00:13:31.760
fort, and the team exploring the grounds. Ray puts&nbsp;
on an old naval uniform he finds and lays down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.760 --> 00:13:37.480
in a bed before the ghost of a beautiful woman&nbsp;
enters the room. Following a brief unseen sex act&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.480 --> 00:13:42.080
that somehow did manage to make it into the final&nbsp;
movie, Winston is seen knocking on the door to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.080 --> 00:13:46.920
room, wondering what's going on. Given the fact&nbsp;
that the most risque portion of the scene actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.920 --> 00:13:52.400
wound up in the theatrical cut, it's likely that&nbsp;
the full extended scene was simply cut for time.

00:13:52.400 --> 00:13:56.600
The culmination of a decade of stories,&nbsp;
Avengers: Infinity War brought together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.600 --> 00:14:00.760
just about every character in the MCU to&nbsp;
battle Thanos, the big bad who wanted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.760 --> 00:14:05.920
erase half of all life in the universe.&nbsp;
With such a broad, sweeping story to tell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.920 --> 00:14:10.440
Infinity War had a lot to pack in, and some&nbsp;
reviewers inevitably felt it was a little long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.440 --> 00:14:15.760
and overstuffed. But it could have been worse:&nbsp;
According to Thanos' creator, Jim Starlin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:15.760 --> 00:14:21.320
the original plan was actually to open the movie&nbsp;
with an additional 45-minute action sequence.

00:14:21.320 --> 00:14:23.680
Instead of beginning with&nbsp;
an attack on Thor's ship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.680 --> 00:14:28.080
Infinity War would have begun with Thanos'&nbsp;
assault on the planet Xandar. He would have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.080 --> 00:14:31.160
decimated the entire planet in&nbsp;
his search for the power stone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.160 --> 00:14:36.480
which is held under protection by the Nova Corps.&nbsp;
In an interview with Collider, Starlin explained:

00:14:36.480 --> 00:14:39.200
"There was a whole sequence of him&nbsp;
getting the first gem and they had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.200 --> 00:14:42.800
to cut that out. They shot it, but they&nbsp;
never wanted to spend the money on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.800 --> 00:14:46.840
effects and they didn't want the movie&nbsp;
to be as long as the second one was."

00:14:46.840 --> 00:14:51.120
Starlin seems to feel that the filmmakers&nbsp;
may have regretted their decision. He added:

00:14:51.120 --> 00:14:54.920
"They didn't realize it was going&nbsp;
to be quite the hit it was."

00:14:54.920 --> 00:14:59.840
Another '80s icon, Back to the Future sends&nbsp;
Michael J. Fox's Marty McFly hurtling back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.840 --> 00:15:04.040
to the '50s. Marty soon discovers&nbsp;
that his father is a total dweeb,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.040 --> 00:15:07.920
and when his mother falls in love with him,&nbsp;
he's forced to help his dad get the girl to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.920 --> 00:15:12.600
save his own existence. The film hammers&nbsp;
home what a loser George is by showing him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.600 --> 00:15:17.668
being bullied by tough guy Biff — but one&nbsp;
deleted scene makes George look even worse.

00:15:17.668 --> 00:15:19.824
"Great Scott!"

00:15:19.824 --> 00:15:24.400
During the Enchantment Under the Sea dance at the&nbsp;
climax of the movie, George is preparing to go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:24.400 --> 00:15:29.440
through with his plan to "rescue" Lorraine from a&nbsp;
staged encounter with Marty. Nervous about acting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:29.440 --> 00:15:33.760
at the right moment, he seeks out a phone booth&nbsp;
and calls the operator to get the time, only to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.760 --> 00:15:39.240
locked inside by Mark, the redheaded class clown&nbsp;
who later cuts into his dance with Lorraine. With&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.240 --> 00:15:43.400
a prop trident holding the door closed, George&nbsp;
is then discovered by Principal Strickland, who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:43.400 --> 00:15:48.840
scolds him for being a slacker… but doesn't let&nbsp;
him out. Likely cut for time or pacing reasons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.840 --> 00:15:54.000
this sequence actually explains why George&nbsp;
didn't make it to the car in time to stop Marty.

00:15:54.000 --> 00:15:58.560
Most films these days include plenty of deleted&nbsp;
scenes once they make their way to home video.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.800 --> 00:16:01.960
Fans love to go over the various bits&nbsp;
of the movie they never got to see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:01.960 --> 00:16:07.280
but they're typically superfluous moments and it's&nbsp;
usually easy to understand why they were removed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:07.280 --> 00:16:11.160
Sometimes, though, there's a scene that leaves&nbsp;
you wondering why it wasn't in the movie — and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:11.160 --> 00:16:16.320
that's the case with a deleted scene from Rian&nbsp;
Johnson's 2012 sci-fi action movie Looper.

00:16:16.320 --> 00:16:21.040
The film stars Bruce Willis as Joe, an aging&nbsp;
hitman who uses time travel to dispose of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.040 --> 00:16:25.800
targets. When he's due for retirement and his&nbsp;
superiors send him back in time to be killed by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:25.800 --> 00:16:31.160
his younger self, old Joe manages to escape.&nbsp;
At one point, young Joe hides out at a house&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:31.160 --> 00:16:37.000
belonging to a woman named Sara — and, soon after&nbsp;
he arrives, a mafia thug shows up looking for him.

00:16:37.000 --> 00:16:41.400
After a tense, drawn-out search of the house, Sara&nbsp;
gives the goon a tour of the basement where Joe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:41.400 --> 00:16:46.840
is hiding, and we're sure it's going to lead to a&nbsp;
showdown. The suspense is palpable as the mobster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:46.840 --> 00:16:51.560
stalks the room… only for us to realize that Joe&nbsp;
is hiding beneath the floor, where he is able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:51.560 --> 00:16:57.880
to escape notice. The choice to leave out this&nbsp;
incredibly tense scene is a real head scratcher.

00:16:57.880 --> 00:17:01.240
Following Star Trek: Nemesis, the&nbsp;
Star Trek franchise took a hiatus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:01.240 --> 00:17:05.880
from the big screen — but it came roaring&nbsp;
back with a new look in J.J. Abrams' 2009&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:05.880 --> 00:17:11.320
reboot. The sequel to that movie, Star Trek&nbsp;
Into Darkness, centered once again on Khan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:11.320 --> 00:17:13.880
this time played by Sherlock&nbsp;
star Benedict Cumberbatch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:13.880 --> 00:17:18.787
who is an even more ruthless and cunning version&nbsp;
of the villain than that seen in Wrath of Khan.

00:17:18.787 --> 00:17:22.498
"Khaaaaan!"

00:17:22.720 --> 00:17:26.560
This time around, he is revealed to have been&nbsp;
manipulated by a secret black ops division&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:26.560 --> 00:17:32.400
of Starfleet called Section 31, led by Admiral&nbsp;
Marcus. While the final film doesn't reveal the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:32.400 --> 00:17:37.720
truth about who is manipulating him until later,&nbsp;
an early deleted scene almost gives it away.

00:17:37.720 --> 00:17:42.080
This moment comes in the form of a video&nbsp;
communication to Marcus from Thomas Harewood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:42.080 --> 00:17:47.280
who is about to detonate a bomb in London at&nbsp;
Khan's behest. The scene shows Marcus receiving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:47.280 --> 00:17:51.400
the transmission in his office, with Harewood&nbsp;
informing him that the impending destruction will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:51.400 --> 00:17:55.760
be a message from Khan. Ultimately, though,&nbsp;
producers cut the scene because it revealed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:55.760 --> 00:18:01.480
a little too much about the players involved. In&nbsp;
the film's commentary, editor Mary Jo Markey said:

00:18:01.480 --> 00:18:04.880
"We felt that it was very important&nbsp;
not to introduce the admiral in any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:04.880 --> 00:18:08.880
way or tip our hand about who he&nbsp;
would become later on in the film,.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:08.880 --> 00:18:13.600
We realized to hold him back for a&nbsp;
long time would be more shocking."

00:18:13.600 --> 00:18:18.520
The MCU is littered with juicy deleted scenes,&nbsp;
but few are as jaw-dropping as one moment cut&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:18.520 --> 00:18:22.920
from Doctor Strange in the Multiverse of&nbsp;
Madness. The film sees Stephen Strange&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:22.920 --> 00:18:27.640
face off against Wanda Maximoff, who has turned&nbsp;
twisted and vengeful after losing Vision and her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:27.640 --> 00:18:32.200
children. Determined to find an alternate reality&nbsp;
where she and her family can live in peace,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:32.200 --> 00:18:37.280
Wanda traverses the multiverse itself — and&nbsp;
basically slaughters everyone in her path.

00:18:37.280 --> 00:18:41.880
Multiverse of Madness has Wanda taking the&nbsp;
proverbial ax to the likes of Mr. Fantastic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:41.880 --> 00:18:46.800
Professor Charles Xavier, and Black Bolt, but&nbsp;
a deleted scene further shows just how vicious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:46.800 --> 00:18:51.400
she has really become. Not long after the&nbsp;
movie's release, one of the film's artists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:51.400 --> 00:18:55.440
Jimmy Simser, released storyboards depicting&nbsp;
the moment when Scarlet Witch arrives at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:55.440 --> 00:19:00.080
Kamar Taj to get her hands on America&nbsp;
Chavez. In this unseen version, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:00.080 --> 00:19:05.280
Wanda isn't alone — as she brings with&nbsp;
her the decapitated head of Baron Mordo.

00:19:05.280 --> 00:19:10.640
1989's Bill &amp; Ted's Excellent Adventure is a&nbsp;
weird and wild story that sees the two friends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:10.640 --> 00:19:15.600
enlist famous faces from history to help them get&nbsp;
a passing grade in school to save the future. Over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:15.600 --> 00:19:18.880
the years, a number of deleted scenes from&nbsp;
the movie have cropped up here and there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:18.880 --> 00:19:23.354
and in 2020, one of the film's stars dug&nbsp;
into the vault to reveal yet another.

00:19:23.354 --> 00:19:25.754
"Excellent!"

00:19:25.920 --> 00:19:30.360
Posting on X, formerly known as Twitter, Alex&nbsp;
Winter showed off images of a sequence early&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:30.360 --> 00:19:34.800
in the film in which Bill and Ted make their way&nbsp;
home from school. The sequence would have included&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:34.800 --> 00:19:39.960
a bizarre musical number that fans ultimately&nbsp;
never got to see. Winter told Rolling Stone:

00:19:39.960 --> 00:19:43.760
"The opening was an almost surreal rock&nbsp;
number where Keanu and I are at a bus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:43.760 --> 00:19:46.840
stop waiting for the school bus in the&nbsp;
morning and we start to air-guitar and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:46.840 --> 00:19:50.720
rock out. And it turned into a whole&nbsp;
dance routine. Then it ends up with us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:50.720 --> 00:19:54.080
kind of getting into a skirmish with the&nbsp;
school jocks and you meet our characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:54.080 --> 00:19:58.520
that way. All of that stuff was shot&nbsp;
and none of it made it into the film."

00:19:58.520 --> 00:20:02.080
A box office juggernaut, E.T. the&nbsp;
Extra-Terrestrial was nominated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:02.080 --> 00:20:05.880
for nine Academy Awards including&nbsp;
best picture and best director;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:05.880 --> 00:20:11.080
it won best original score and three additional&nbsp;
technical awards. The story of a strange alien&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:11.080 --> 00:20:14.920
visitor who is befriended by a pair of&nbsp;
young children named Elliott and Gertie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:14.920 --> 00:20:19.880
E.T. remains one of the most beloved family&nbsp;
movies of all time. Yet, a few deleted scenes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:19.880 --> 00:20:23.640
that have shown up over the years might&nbsp;
have totally changed the tone of the film.

00:20:23.640 --> 00:20:27.720
In addition to a comedic moment that didn't&nbsp;
make the cut because the E.T. puppet just wasn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:27.720 --> 00:20:31.640
convincing enough, there's one other scene&nbsp;
that seems awfully out of place in a movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:31.640 --> 00:20:37.040
aimed at kids and families. Popping up decades&nbsp;
later thanks to some behind-the-scenes stills,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:37.040 --> 00:20:40.360
it shows Elliott's mother Mary&nbsp;
lying face down but topless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:40.360 --> 00:20:45.520
asleep in bed late at night. As she slumbers,&nbsp;
the alien creeps into her bedroom and silently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:45.520 --> 00:20:50.600
observes her. Though probably intended to show&nbsp;
E.T. learning about humans, the images give off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:50.600 --> 00:20:55.459
a very unsettling vibe — one that might well&nbsp;
have disturbed the families in the audience.

